From 4cfd86639cc95440718f575e4d458ca309efdb7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Panagiotis Moustafellos <pmoust@nofire.ai>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 04:29:32 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] clocksource: Add the hvi-time paravirtual clock and timer
 driver

A guest of the hvi VMM can be checkpointed on one host and restored on
another whose counter runs at a different frequency: 24 MHz on Apple
silicon under Hypervisor.framework, 54 MHz on a Raspberry Pi 5 under
KVM. The arch timer driver caches the boot frequency in its
clocksource, sched_clock, clockevent and the delay loop, so every
kernel clock runs at the wrong rate after such a move.

hvi exposes an "hvi,time-v1" device instead: a pvclock page that maps
CNTVCT_EL0 to guest nanoseconds (base_counter, base_ns, mult, shift
under a generation seqlock) and that hvi republishes on restore, plus a
per-CPU one-shot timer that takes an absolute guest nanosecond deadline
and raises a per-CPU PPI. Guest nanoseconds stop while the guest is
stopped, so CLOCK_MONOTONIC stays continuous across a restore.

The driver registers a 1 GHz clocksource and sched_clock on the page
and a per-CPU oneshot clockevent on the timer, all at rating 500 so
they win over the arch timer. Each CPU uses the timer block of its
vCPU index, MPIDR_EL1.Aff0. Disarming the timer also clears a latched
expiry, because the PPI is level and would otherwise stay high.

When hvi signals a new generation, the interrupt handler rescales
loops_per_jiffy at once, so udelay() is never short on a faster
counter, and a work item moves CLOCK_REALTIME and CLOCK_BOOTTIME on by
the time the guest was stopped and touches the lockup watchdogs. A
core initcall fixes loops_per_jiffy after the boot calibration. An
fs_initcall_sync sets the wall clock when nothing has. It runs after
timekeeping has switched to hvi-time, because a clock set while
timekeeping still reads the jiffies clocksource is forwarded in whole
jiffies at the switch and starts up to one jiffy off the host.

The driver registers a synthetic 54 MHz counter with
arm64_set_el0_counter(). With the previous patch, EL0 counter access
then stays disabled and userspace reads of CNTVCT_EL0 and CNTFRQ_EL0
trap to that counter. /sys/kernel/hvi_time/ exposes the page state for
tests.

The driver must be built in: TIMER_OF_DECLARE runs from time_init(),
sched_clock_register() is __init and timekeeping_inject_sleeptime64()
is not exported and exists only with PM_SLEEP and RTC_HCTOSYS. It adds
CPUHP_AP_HVI_TIME_STARTING so that each CPU gets its clockevent before
it enables interrupts, as the arch timer does. There is no device tree
binding for "hvi,time-v1"; the series is not meant for upstream as is.

Signed-off-by: Panagiotis Moustafellos <pmoust@nofire.ai>
---
 drivers/clocksource/Kconfig    |  20 ++
 drivers/clocksource/Makefile   |   1 +
 drivers/clocksource/hvi-time.c | 607 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 include/linux/cpuhotplug.h     |   1 +
 4 files changed, 629 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/clocksource/hvi-time.c

diff --git a/drivers/clocksource/Kconfig b/drivers/clocksource/Kconfig
index abfc2aa85..c2f90dda0 100644
--- a/drivers/clocksource/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/clocksource/Kconfig
@@ -755,4 +755,24 @@ config EP93XX_TIMER
 	  Enables support for the Cirrus Logic timer block
 	  EP93XX.
 
+config HVI_TIME
+	bool "hvi paravirtual clock and timer"
+	depends on ARM64 && OF
+	depends on PM_SLEEP && RTC_HCTOSYS
+	select TIMER_OF
+	help
+	  Clocksource, sched_clock and per-CPU clockevent for guests of the
+	  hvi VMM. They read a pvclock page that the VMM republishes when the
+	  guest is restored on another host, so guest time stays continuous
+	  across hosts with different counter frequencies. EL0 access to the
+	  counter is kept disabled, so userspace reads of CNTVCT_EL0 and
+	  CNTFRQ_EL0 trap and see a fixed 54 MHz counter.
+
+	  Restores move CLOCK_BOOTTIME on with
+	  timekeeping_inject_sleeptime64(), which exists only with PM_SLEEP
+	  and RTC_HCTOSYS.
+
+	  Say N unless the kernel runs under hvi with the hvi,time-v1
+	  device in its device tree.
+
 endmenu
